# The '98 intro block, $C000-$E9FF (NEZSEd.B sectors 2-43, read there by the REM loader, src/basic98/basic.asm):
# the annotations kit/mkgame.py --part src/intro98/intro.json turns into src/intro98/*.asm. The format is kit/mkgame.py's (class
# Annotations); the code is what recording r98 ran in the intro window, frames 2048 to 4500
# (work/stage2/intro98/w-intro/l1.json), widened by the kit's walk. Counts quoted below ("entered N times") are that
# window's L1 counts. The player's names and most of its descriptions start from Advanced Nether Earth's
# src/music/pt3-player.asm (the same player, byte for byte but for the two places named in the module's header).

@module pt3player ; PT 3 Player v1.0 by Nick of GDC: the compiled tune's player
@about The player Pro Tracker 3.x writes in front of a compiled tune ("PT 3 Player v1.0 by Nick of GDC" at +$0B), with the module right after it at +$E21 ($CE21, module pt3tune). The block starts with it: the REM loader reads the block to $C000 and the intro (IntroMain) CALLs Pt3Init at $C000 once (r98 frame 2048), its interrupt IntroInterrupt CALLs Pt3PlayEntry every frame (2155 times, frames 2049-4203) and IntroEnd CALLs Pt3StopEntry once (frame 4203). Entries: Pt3Init +0 (LD HL,Pt3Module; the module set up, every channel muted, the AY written silent; DI, returns with interrupts off), Pt3PlayEntry +5 (JP Pt3Play: one frame; interrupts off, it uses SP as a data pointer and IYL as a scratch, IY = $5C3A again on return), Pt3StopEntry +8 (JP Pt3Stop: the three volumes 0, the registers written once).
@about How it works (a frame): 1. the three channel ticks (ChAMute..., ChBMute..., ChCMute...): sample line, volume (VolumeTable), noise / envelope offset, mixer, ornament, tone (NoteTable) + slide, on-off; the envelope slide; 2. WriteAy: registers 0-12 from the AY block at a page start (L = the register number), 13 only when a new envelope type was set (EnvType); 3. the pattern reading, spread over three frames by RowFrame: channel A's row two frames before the row, B's one frame before, C's on the row's frame. A and B are decoded into a staging area (the operands of the blocks from ChANewNoteJr on, each block skipped by a JR / JP whose displacement the decoder clears), C applies its row at once; the staged A and B blocks run right after C's decode, so all three channels change on the same frame. RowFrame is reloaded with the delay there: the player needs a delay of 3 or more (COOL WINTER's is 5).
@about The channel bytes (the decoders ChADecode, ChBDecode, ChCDecode; B = $10, C = $20 step the accumulator through the ranges with ADD and the carry): $F0-$FF ornament n + the next byte: sample * 2 (envelope off, ornament from its start); $D1-$EF sample n-$D0 (for the next note); $D0 the row ends, nothing new; $C1-$CF volume n-$C0; $C0 sound off, the row ends; $B2-$BF envelope type n-$B1 + period (2 bytes, high first); $B1 + byte: rows to skip; $B0 envelope off; $50-$AF note n-$50, the row ends; $40-$4F ornament n-$40; $11-$1F envelope type n-$10 + period (2 bytes) + sample * 2; $10 envelope off + sample * 2; $20-$3F noise base n-$20 in channel B (A and C take them as $11-$1F, a quirk of this player); $01-$09 an effect: its routine's address (ChAEffects...) is pushed and runs after the row's note, its parameters follow the row's bytes (1 gliss: delay, step word; 2 portamento: delay, distance word, step word; 3 sample offset; 4 ornament offset; 5 on-off: off frames, on frames; 8 envelope slide: delay, step word; 9 delay; 6 and 7 do nothing: $0052, a RET in the 48 K BASIC ROM, which is paged in while the intro runs, $7FFD = $10); $00 at the start of channel A's row: the pattern ends, the next position.
@about Against the signature R/signatures/sound/ay-players/pro-tracker-3/nick-gdc-1.0 (Advanced Nether Earth's copy at the same address $C000): 3557 of the player's 3617 bytes are equal; 60 differ, in two places. (1) VolumeTable ($C100-$C1FF, 50 bytes): here it is Ivan Roshin's volume table in its Pro Tracker 3.3x-3.4x form (row v, column l: v = 0 gives 0, else l * (v + 1) / 16 rounded down), the signature's is the 3.5x+ form (l * (v * 17 + (v > 6)) / 256 rounded to nearest). The module after it says "ProTracker 3.3 compilation": the tracker writes the table that fits the version it compiles for (Bulba's player builds the same two forms at init, chosen by the version digit); this player never builds it, it plays with the one in the file. (2) AyPad ($C2F6-$C2FF, 10 bytes): the text "-VORONEZH-" here, zeros in the signature; no code reads these bytes (no read in the window). Everything else, the code and the note table included, is the same bytes; the variables in the operands and SamplePtrs / OrnamentPtrs are zero in the file in both (Pt3Init fills them: 130 bytes of $C048-$C0FF change at frame 2048).
@about Known bug (from the code, as in the signature's copy): channel B's on-off effect (ChBEffOnOff) sets ChBOnOffJp to jump to itself, so the player would hang on a row that applies it to channel B; the window played COOL WINTER for 2155 frames with no hang.
$C000 c Pt3Init ; the module set up: LD HL,Pt3Module, into InitModule
> Entry +0 of the compiled player. HL = Pt3Module ($CE21, the tune right after the player), then InitModule: the pointers, the delay, every channel muted and the AY written silent (through InitState into Pt3Stop). Returns with interrupts off (InitModule's DI). Called once by IntroMain (r98 frame 2048).
$C005 c Pt3PlayEntry ; entry +5: JP Pt3Play, one frame of the tune
> Called by IntroInterrupt every frame (2155 times, r98 frames 2049-4203), with interrupts off.
$C008 c Pt3StopEntry ; entry +8: JP Pt3Stop, the AY silent
> Called once by IntroEnd (frame 4203) when a key ended the intro.
$C00B t PlayerId ; " PT 3 Player v1.0 by Nick of GDC " (33 bytes, never read)
$C02C c InitModule ; HL = the module
> HL = the module. The module address into every reader's operand, the delay, the position pointers (the start and the loop), the pattern table, the 48 sample and ornament pointers (offsets + the address) copied to SamplePtrs / OrnamentPtrs; then InitState.
$C039 ; +100: the delay
$C047 InitPositions
$C04A ; +201: the position list
$C05E InitPatterns
$C068 ; 32 sample + 16 ornament pointers
$C06A InitPointerLoop
$C06B InitPointerBase
$C078 InitSp
$C07B ; volume 15 (its row of VolumeTable)
$C09B ; RowFrame: A decoded on the next frame
$C0A0 b SamplePtrs 16 ; 32 sample pointers (module address added by InitModule); at a page offset $A0: the decoders index ...
> 32 sample pointers (module address added by InitModule); at a page offset $A0: the decoders index it by the low byte
$C0E0 b OrnamentPtrs 16 ; 16 ornament pointers, right after the samples (page offset $E0)
> 16 ornament pointers, right after the samples (page offset $E0)
$C100 b VolumeTable 16 ; 16 x 16 bytes at a page start: [channel volume * 16 + sample level] -> the AY volume (3.3x-3.4x form)
> 16 x 16 bytes at a page start: [channel volume * 16 + sample level] -> the AY volume. Pro Tracker 3.3x-3.4x's form of Ivan Roshin's table (row v, column l: 0 for v = 0, else l * (v + 1) / 16 rounded down; row 1 = 0 x 8, 1 x 8; row 2 = 0 x 6, 1 x 5, 2 x 5), written by the tracker for this 3.3 module; the signature's copy holds the 3.5x+ form (50 of the 256 bytes differ). Its first byte (0) is also the "end of pattern" InitState points channel A at.
$C200 b NoteTable 16 ; 96 tone periods (words, notes C-1 .. B-8 of the tracker), at a page start: PT3's tone table #1 ...
> 96 tone periods (words, notes C-1 .. B-8 of the tracker), at a page start: PT3's tone table #1 (Sound Tracker's, $0EF8 for the lowest note), the only one this player has. The effect tables follow it in the same page (the decoders index them by the low byte).
$C2C0 w ChAEffects ; The effect routines of the three channels, effects 1-9 (6 and 7 unused: $0052, a RET in the 48 K ...
> The effect routines of the three channels, effects 1-9 (6 and 7 unused: $0052, a RET in the 48 K ROM).
$C2D2 ChBEffects
$C2E4 ChCEffects
$C2F6 t AyPad ; "-VORONEZH-": 10 bytes up to the page start the AY block needs, never read
> The text "-VORONEZH-" (the town of the player's authors, Nick of GDC: "Golden Disk, Voronezh" in ANE's source of the player; who put it here is not known; ANE's copy holds zeros here). WriteAy needs the AY block at a page start, so these bytes are padding: no code reads or writes them (none in the window).
$C300 b AyToneA 2 ; The AY block, at a page start (WriteAy uses L as the register number): registers 0-12
> The AY block, at a page start (WriteAy uses L as the register number): registers 0-12.
$C302 AyToneB
$C304 AyToneC
$C306 AyUnused ; registers 6 and 7: WriteAy takes IXH and IXL instead
$C308 AyVolumeA
$C309 AyVolumeB
$C30A AyVolumeC
$C30B AyEnvPeriod
$C30D AyEnvTypeUnused ; register 13: written from EnvType, not from here
$C30E c InitState ; the channels' start values: RowFrame 3, every row skip counter 1 (decode on the first frame), the ...
> the channels' start values: RowFrame 3, every row skip counter 1 (decode on the first frame), the channels muted, the envelope masks off, RowCounter -1, channel A's pattern pointer at a 0 byte (VolumeTable's first) so that the first frame reads position 0. Falls into Pt3Stop.
$C340 c Pt3Stop ; the three volumes 0, then WriteAy with RestoreIy patched to RET (only the AY write)
> the three volumes 0, then WriteAy with RestoreIy patched to RET (only the AY write).
$C358 c Pt3Play ; one frame
> one frame. IYL = 0 (the envelope offset of the samples), IX = the noise of the last frame (IXH) and the mixer 0 (IXL); SP saved in PlaySp (the ticks read with POP). NoiseMixer+3 = IXH: the noise is kept from frame to frame.
$C35B NoiseMixer
$C363 c ChAMute ; Channel A, one tick: the sample line, the volume, the noise or envelope offset, the mixer bits, the ...
> Channel A, one tick: the sample line, the volume, the noise or envelope offset, the mixer bits, the ornament, the tone and its slide. Runs every frame; the channel's state lives in the operands of these instructions (each label names the value its operand holds). Out: the tone in the AY block, the volume in AyVolumeA (0 when muted), the mixer bits ORed into IXL, the noise into IXH, the envelope offset added to IYL.
> ChAMute: 0 while the channel sounds, $FF when it is off ($C0, before the first note, or the on-off effect's off phase).
